30.4  Remotes: fetch, pull and push

TL;DR
  - A remote is another copy of the repository, usually origin on GitHub or
    GitLab. Your copy is complete: most commands work offline.
  - fetch downloads new commits without touching your branches; pull is fetch,
    then merge (or rebase) into your branch; push sends yours.
  - origin/main is your last fetched view of the remote's main, not a live one.

      git remote -v
      git fetch && git status        # ahead or behind origin?
      git branch -vv                 # each branch's upstream and distance

On your machine
  Files     /etc/gitconfig, ~/.gitconfig
  Identity  name and email set
  Settings  init.defaultbranch = main
            pull.rebase = true
            core.autocrlf = input
            credential.helper = store (passwords in plain text in
            ~/.git-credentials)
  Aliases   st, lg
  Includes  1 more file, read under conditions (include, includeIf)

Pitfalls
  - "Updates were rejected": the remote has commits you don't. Pull (or fetch
    and rebase) first, then push again.
  - "Need to specify how to reconcile divergent branches": choose once,
    git config --global pull.rebase true (or false to merge).
  - Prefer --force-with-lease to --force: it refuses if the remote moved since
    your last fetch, instead of erasing someone's commits.
  - push.autoSetupRemote = true saves the -u origin BRANCH on a new branch's
    first push.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 30.4 --why

